  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q:pains-first-call",
      "question": "On the first call with the last ten customers you closed, what did they say was broken, in their words? Write the sentence, not the category.",
      "why": "Every statement in this map is built from strangers' public reviews. One sentence from a real first call outranks all of it and tells us which pain is the one that opens deals.",
      "would_change": "pains[].statement and pains[].confidence"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-incumbent-mirror",
      "question": "Which of these eight pains do prospects bring in from their previous tool, and which are objections they raise about us? Mark each one.",
      "why": "Almost every quote here comes from reviewers of this company's own product, which is the loudest public record available. That makes the map double as an objection list, and downstream steps need to know which row is which before positioning or angles are written.",
      "would_change": "pains[].statement, pains[].type and which rows the positioning step treats as objections"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-personal-rung",
      "question": "When a customer tells you the tool let them down - the feature was in the next tier, or it did not do what they were sold - what do they say it cost them personally? A review, a reorganisation, a bad quarter?",
      "why": "Three pains carry a null personal rung because no public source shows one. The personal rung is what the angles step writes hooks from, and inventing it would put words in a buyer's mouth.",
      "would_change": "pains[].shape.personal on pain:the-thing-i-need-is-one-tier-up, pain:sold-one-thing-got-another and pain:bill-grows-with-the-database"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-agency-identity",
      "question": "Of the reviewers who work at marketing and advertising firms, are they partners running portals for clients, or in-house marketers at an agency using it for themselves?",
      "why": "seg:agency is assigned in three pains on the strength of the reviewer's industry only. If these are in-house marketers rather than partners, the agency segment has almost no evidence in this map and the ICP step should not treat it as swept.",
      "would_change": "pains[].segments and pains[].confidence on the three rows carrying seg:agency"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-multi-brand",
      "question": "Do partners and multi-brand customers tell you that keeping each client or brand separate costs them extra subscription, and what do they do about it today?",
      "why": "One source says organising each brand separately became difficult without increasing subscription costs. One source is an anecdote, so it is not a row in this map, but if partners say it, it is likely the agency segment's sharpest pain.",
      "would_change": "pains[] - would add a row for multi-brand or multi-client separation, with seg:agency"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-duplicates",
      "question": "Do your customers complain about duplicate records and contact data quality, and at what point in their use does it start?",
      "why": "One reviewer names duplicate creation and a second mentions contact management, both on the same page, so it fails the two-independent-sources bar and cannot be a row. Data quality is the kind of pain that is felt daily and posted about rarely.",
      "would_change": "pains[] - would add a data-quality row pointing at feat:data"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-report-moment",
      "question": "What is the specific report a customer asked for and could not build, and what meeting was it for?",
      "why": "The reporting pain is the only one at medium confidence where the quotes describe a product limit rather than a moment. Naming the meeting turns it into a workflow the angles step can write to.",
      "would_change": "pains[].workflow and pains[].shape.personal on pain:cant-build-the-report-that-proves-it"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-churn-reason",
      "question": "What did the last ten accounts that left say on the way out, and how many of them named the contract terms rather than the product?",
      "why": "The public record is dominated by contract and renewal complaints. Churn reasons would show whether that reflects the customer base or only the subset angry enough to post.",
      "would_change": "pains[].confidence on pain:cant-get-out-of-the-contract and pain:sold-one-thing-got-another"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-manual-steps",
      "question": "Which manual step do customers describe as normal - the weekly export, the CSV fix, the re-upload - that they have stopped calling a problem?",
      "why": "The one latent pain here rests on two flat descriptions of a workaround. A customer naming their own routine would confirm it and give it a personal rung.",
      "would_change": "pains[].shape.personal and pains[].confidence on pain:hand-carried-data-between-systems"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:pains-individual-layer",
      "question": "What does choosing this platform do for the person who chose it - their standing, their next role, their review?",
      "why": "Coverage check: no pain in this map inverts an element in the individual layer (reputational assurance, growth and development, reduced anxiety), because public reviewers write as companies rather than as people. That layer is usually where the strongest angles come from.",
      "would_change": "pains[] - would add an individual-layer row, and pains[].shape.personal across the map"
    }
  ],
